<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  RM_WetBulbProtection.xml               EnvironmentalHazards - GREENTIDE_MECHANICS_2 M1

  The apparel stat RM_GameCondition_WetBulb sums across a pawn's worn
  apparel (RM_GameCondition_WetBulb.cs, SumApparelProtection - an
  "Apparel"-category StatDef has no vanilla auto-aggregation onto a pawn
  stat; ArmorUtility is the only vanilla reader of that category and reads
  per-apparel, not per-pawn, so the condition does the summing itself).

  ParentName="ArmorRatingBase" cribs the same abstract vanilla base
  RM_ArmorRating_Scald.xml already uses in this same mod (category Apparel,
  defaultBaseValue 0, minValue 0, hideAtValue 0, toStringStyle PercentOne
  all inherited unedited) - maxValue overridden to 1 here since this stat
  is read as a 0..1 protection fraction (unlike an armor rating, which can
  exceed 1 via quality/stuff bonuses), not because 2 (armor's own default)
  is wrong, just irrelevant to this stat's own math.

  No StatPart_Stuff entry, same reasoning RM_ArmorRating_Scald.xml gives:
  no vendored Stuff in this repo grants wet-bulb protection, so apparel
  grants it via a flat <statBases> entry on the garment itself (the gear
  item defs own the concrete numbers, not this file).
-->
<Defs>

  <StatDef ParentName="ArmorRatingBase" MayRequire="mandrake.rm.environmentalhazards">
    <defName>RM_WetBulbProtection</defName>
    <label>wet-bulb protection</label>
    <labelForFullStatList>Wet-bulb protection (armor stat)</labelForFullStatList>
    <description>How much this garment slows the wet-bulb overwhelm clock — sealed or wicking gear built for saturated heat, not ordinary insulation. Ordinary shade and cooling gear does nothing against wet-bulb heat; only apparel carrying this stat helps.

Summed across every garment worn at once, then capped. Worn together, enough of it holds the clock indefinitely; on its own, most single pieces only slow it.</description>
    <maxValue>1</maxValue>
    <parts>
      <li Class="StatPart_Quality">
        <factorAwful>0.6</factorAwful>
        <factorPoor>0.8</factorPoor>
        <factorNormal>1</factorNormal>
        <factorGood>1.15</factorGood>
        <factorExcellent>1.3</factorExcellent>
        <factorMasterwork>1.45</factorMasterwork>
        <factorLegendary>1.8</factorLegendary>
      </li>
    </parts>
  </StatDef>

</Defs>
